# PostHog CSS Capture Impacts

> [!IMPORTANT]
> PostHog autocapture reads **computed CSS** to decide whether a click is worth
> capturing. A `div` gets tracked or ignored based on its `cursor` property.
> Changing a style rule in the Webflow Designer can silently turn analytics off.

## The finding

`posthog-js` autocapture captures clicks on a fixed set of tags:

```
a, button, form, input, select, textarea, label
```

For **any other tag** — a `div`, `span`, `li` — it falls back to a heuristic:
it calls `getComputedStyle()` and checks whether `cursor` resolves to `pointer`.
If yes, it treats the element as interactive and captures the click. If no, the
click is dropped.

That is the whole mechanism. There is no magic — `window.getComputedStyle()` is
an ordinary DOM API available to any script on the page. What is surprising is
not *how* it reads the CSS, but *that analytics coverage is a function of
presentation styling at all*.

## Verified behaviour

Tested against the exact build in production on Ponsonby Doctors
(`posthog-js` 1.161.5, loaded from the site bundle), using a probe page that
intercepts `capture()` so nothing is sent.

Six `div` elements, identical except for cursor styling and position:

| Element's own computed `cursor` | Ancestor `cursor` | Captured? |
|---|---|---|
| `pointer` | `default` | Yes |
| `default` | `pointer` | Yes |
| `pointer` (inherited) | `pointer` | Yes |
| `default` | `default` | No |
| `default` | `auto` | No |
| `pointer` | `auto` (body) | Yes |

**Rule:** a click is captured if the clicked element **or any ancestor** has a
computed `cursor` of `pointer`.

Two consequences fall out of that:

- `cursor` is an **inherited** CSS property, so setting `pointer` on a wrapper
  makes every descendant capturable by default.
- Explicitly overriding a child back to `cursor: default` does **not** exclude
  it — the ancestor is checked independently and still qualifies.

## Why this matters here

The Ponsonby Doctors nav autocomplete toggle is a `div`
(`.dropdown-toggle-2.w-dropdown-toggle`). Webflow styles it `cursor: pointer`,
so its clicks are captured today. That is the only reason "user opened the
search dropdown" is trackable without code.

Nobody chose that. It is a side effect of a Webflow style default.

## Risks

- **Silent breakage.** A designer changes a hover style, sets
  `cursor: default`, and an event stream stops. No error, no console warning,
  no build failure. It surfaces weeks later as a gap in a chart.
- **Silent over-capture.** Any decorative wrapper styled `cursor: pointer`
  makes its entire subtree capturable. On a site with generous pointer styling
  this inflates autocapture volume, which is billed per event.
- **Untestable from the code.** Nothing in the HTML or the JS says "this is
  tracked". The answer lives in a compiled stylesheet.
- **Cross-team invisibility.** The person who can break tracking (a designer in
  Webflow) has no way to know they are touching analytics.

## Open questions

- Exact traversal order in the minified source. Reading the bundle suggested
  only ancestors are checked; the empirical results show the element's own
  cursor also qualifies. The behaviour table above is authoritative — the code
  reading is not yet reconciled. Worth checking against the unminified
  `posthog-js` source.
- Does the same heuristic apply to **heatmaps** and **dead-click capture**, or
  only to interaction autocapture? Both are enabled on this project.
- Is there a supported way to mark an element as interactive **explicitly**,
  independent of styling? `css_selector_allowlist` exists but is a narrowing
  AND-gate — setting it restricts autocapture site-wide rather than adding to
  it, so it is not a fit.
- What is the cost exposure? Worth counting how many `cursor: pointer`
  elements exist across the site.

## Mitigation to consider

Do not rely on the heuristic for anything that matters. For events that must be
reliable, either put them on a real `<a>` or `<button>`, or capture them
explicitly in code. Treat cursor-derived capture as a convenience that may
disappear without notice.

## Reproduction

Probe page used for the tests:

```
scratchpad/phtest.html
```

It loads `posthog-js@1.161.5`, points `api_host` at a dead address, and
monkey-patches `posthog.capture` to record calls instead of sending them. Safe
to re-run; sends nothing.
